"A tenant isolation diagram shows which resources tenants share, which they own separately, and where tenant context is enforced.\n\nUse when: Review a pooled, siloed, or mixed SaaS isolation design. Explain how tenant identity reaches storage and authorization boundaries.\n\nAvoid when: Only general service deployment without tenant-specific enforcement matters.\n\nVisual grammar: Tenant markers identify distinct logical customers using synthetic names. Resource groups distinguish pooled and tenant-dedicated instances. Labeled paths carry tenant context and identify enforcement points.\n\nBuild: Choose representative tenants and the resource-sharing model. Place compute and storage within their actual sharing boundaries. Trace tenant context through access decisions and data selection. Separate tenant paths and pad shared-resource boxes so enforcement notes remain readable.\n\nCheck: Shared infrastructure does not imply shared authorization. Tenant context is derived and validated at documented boundaries. Isolation claims include relevant data, cache, and background-job paths. Tenant routes and boundary labels do not intersect.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a tenant_id column as complete isolation evidence. Assuming dedicated compute automatically isolates every shared dependency.\n\nExample: Tenants Alpha and Beta share an API pool while scoped authorization and keyed storage access preserve their separate data boundaries.\n\nAlternatives: Authorization graph, Deployment diagram."
